Experiment 5(a)
Title: Write a shell script that takes a command line argument and reports on whether it is a directory or not.  

Theory:
In UNIX, a shell script is a program written using shell commands. While executing it, command line arguments can be passed to a shell script.  
$1 represents the first command line argument passed to the script.  
-f checks whether the argument is a regular file.  
-d checks whether the argument is a directory.  

Algorithm:
Start the program.  
Accept a path as a command line argument.  
Check if the argument is a regular file using -f.  
If true, display that it is a file.  
Otherwise, check if it is a directory using -d.  
If true, display that it is a directory.  
Otherwise, display that the given path does not exist.  
Stop.  


Shell script-
#!/bin/bash

if [ -f "$1" ]
then
    echo "$1 is a file"
elif [ -d "$1" ]
then
    echo "$1 is a directory"
else
    echo "$1 does not exist"
fi
